y reasons that they're  struggling to meet demand and increase  productivity this Supply shortage could  easily last for years to come it's not  just manufacturing workers that are  missing either we're seeing shortages  and Logistics side of things as well the  American Trucking associations estimates  that the driver's shortage has increased  from roughly 60 000 before the pandemic  to 80 000 today so not only has the  pandemic reduced number of people who  are producing Goods but also the number  of people who are delivering Goods  foreign  major labor and scaling Miss Supply  chains have also been challenged by  inflation the price of raw materials  have skyrocketed throughout the pandemic  and while they have cooled down from  their peaks there is still a ways to go  take aluminum for example before the  pandemic started it aluminum cost  roughly 1700 per ton at the start of the  pandemic prices briefly cooled down to  1500 before they exploded to nearly four  thousand dollars in February of 2022.  since then prices have come back down to  2 400 but that's still 40 higher than  where they started a similar story can  be seen with Lumber before the pandemic  Lumber generally cost 300 to 400 per  1000 board feet from there Lumber  crashed 250 dollars before rocketing to  an eye watering 1600 fortunately we've  cooled back down to a little over 600  but that's still over 60 higher than  where we started this price volatility  has made everyone within the supply  chain extremely cautious because they  don't want their business to become  unprofitable for example let's say you  own the company that produces chassis  for iPhones you already committed to  making X number of chassis at a given  price but since you made this commitment  the price of aluminum has doubled which  leaves your business unprofitable at  this point you have two options you can  either go to Apple and renegotiate a  higher price which will likely take  weeks if not months or you can wait for  a